<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karluks</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Karluks were a Turkic people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In 744 CE, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basmyls">Basmyls</a>, they seized the Gokturk centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otuken">Ötüken</a> and killed the last Gokturk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>. This ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Karluks were a nomadic Turkic tribal confederacy living around the Black Irtysh and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbagatai-mountains">Tarbagatai Mountains</a>, west of the Altay, between about the 5th and 8th centuries. The first Chinese reference to them, in 644, calls them the Lion Karluks. Three member tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk Khaganate</a> formed the Uch-Karluk, or Three Karluks, union, whose leader first held the title Elteber and later Yabgu. After the khaganate split around 600, the union became subordinate to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>. In 650, at the time of their submission to the Chinese, they had three tribes, and by 665 a Yabgu with a powerful army led the union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 8th century the Karluks rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> and formed a new confederation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basmyls">Basmyls</a>. They remained within the Chinese sphere of influence until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> in 751, when the Arab general Ziyad ibn Salih defeated the Chinese. In 766, after overrunning the Türgesh in Jetisu, they formed a khanate under a Yabghu and moved their capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyab">Suyab</a>. The Karluk Yabgu state sat on the trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and its ruler profited from the slave markets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a>, selling guards to the Abbasid caliphs.</p><p>Karluk independence ended around 840, when the Kyrgyz, who had allied with them against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, took part of Jetisu. Several Muslim historians say that supreme authority among the Turkic tribes then passed to the Karluk leaders, who took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">Khagan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a> grew out of a confederation of Karluks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chigils">Chigils</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaghma">Yagmas</a> and other tribes, and the Kara-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> later ruled the region from about 1130. In 1211 Arslan Khan Karluk killed the Kara-Khitan governor of Kayalik and declared loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Karluks were hunters, nomadic herdsmen and farmers, living in the countryside and in towns built around trading posts on the caravan roads. The Karluk Yabghu state was an association of semi-independent districts and cities, each with its own militia; the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suyab">Suyab</a>, could field 20,000 warriors. Most Karluks first followed Tengrianism. They converted to Nestorian Christianity at the end of the 8th century, and a Karluk yabghu is reported to have become Muslim under Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>. They were the most open of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkic-peoples">Turkic peoples</a> to Muslim culture. The Karluk language became the basis of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-language">Chagatai language</a>, and most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a> descend from Karluk tribes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karluks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 744 CE, the Uyghurs, <b>Karluks</b>, and Basmyls seized Ötüken, killed the last Göktürk qaghan, and sent his head to the Tang court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gokturk-empire">Gokturk Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Most historians connect them with the <b>Karluk</b> confederation, which had dominated the lands of the Chu and Talas rivers since the eighth century, while others favour the Yaghma or the Chigil, tribes that formed the core of their later armies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Repelled at Kashgar in 1131, he extended his authority over Qayaliq and Almaliq, and in 1134 took the Karakhanid city of Balasaghun, which became his capital; the Kankalis, <b>Karluks</b>, Kyrgyz and the Kingdom of Qocho became vassals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Battle of Talas in 751, where a Tang army under Gao Xianzhi was defeated by Abbasid forces after the defection of its <b>Karluk</b> allies, is often cited as marking the limit of Tang westward expansion, and is traditionally linked with the transmission of paper-making westward through captured craftsmen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 742 the Uyghurs, <b>Karluks</b> and Basmyls rebelled against the Second Turkic Khaganate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Karluks</b> lived in the Altai and joined the Uyghurs and Basmyls in overthrowing the Second Turkic Khaganate in 744.</blockquote><a href="/state/karluk-yabgu">Karluk Yabgu</a></li></ul></section>
